{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Are you indeed my brother.</b> The word הכי is not an expression of doubt but of certainty; and so too הכי קרא שמו יעקב, whose Aramaic translation is יאות (i.e., 'indeed')." "<b>And you have served me for nothing.</b> The verse says: you are certainly my brother, and until now you have served me for nothing these few days (חדש ימים), for thus Scripture notes: וישב עמו חדש ימים — and if you wish to remain on from here onward, tell me what your wages shall be. And Jacob answered him: I will serve you seven years." "<b>And one should wonder</b> at this: what was Jacob's intention in beginning to propose to him such a long span of seven years to serve in them? For had Laban begun by saying, 'you shall serve me seven years,' it would have been fitting for Jacob to diminish the time and say that two or three years would suffice him, for that is the term of a hired laborer — how then could he himself initiate this? And it may be explained that the reason for 'I will serve you seven years' is so that Rachel might complete twelve years and one day and become fit for pregnancy, for she was young, five years old, when she came with the flock, and was not fit for a man at all. And that which indicates this is what he said to him: ברחל בתך הקטנה — for it is well known that Rachel was younger than Leah, but the meaning is that on account of her being young I cannot marry her now, and for this reason I will wait for her seven years and serve you. And even though she was fit for intercourse, Jacob — a body of holiness — did not consider being with her until the days were complete and she would be fit for children and he could fulfill the commandment, for he knew of the twelve tribes destined to emerge from him, and what benefit would there be in marrying a woman and being nullified (fruitless)? Therefore he did not wish to marry her until seven years had passed and she would be fit for pregnancy." "<b>And our master</b> Solomon, of blessed memory, explained the addition of saying 'your daughter' — because Laban was established in his eyes as a deceiver, and Jacob thought perhaps he might bring him a woman from the marketplace whose name was Rachel; therefore he needed to say 'your daughter.' And perhaps he might bring him Leah and say, 'this is my daughter'; therefore he added the word 'the younger.' And so Jacob took precaution in this matter lest Laban deceive him, yet in the end Laban deceived him and his precaution availed him nothing, and Laban brought him Leah in place of Rachel."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:17.692664319Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}